\section*{Challenge 14: No Rainbow Cycle in the Hypercube}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Hypercube and coordinate edge-colouring]
The \emph{$n$-dimensional hypercube} $H_n$ is the graph whose vertices are the binary strings of length $n$, with an edge between two strings that differ in exactly one coordinate. The \emph{coordinate edge-colouring} (the standard proper edge-colouring) assigns to each edge the unique coordinate $i \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ in which its two endpoints differ; it uses $n$ colours.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rainbow cycle]
A cycle in an edge-coloured graph is \emph{rainbow} if all of its edges receive pairwise distinct colours.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No rainbow cycle \mbox{\cite{rainbowcycles}}]\label{rainbow}
For every $n \geq 1$, no cycle of the $n$-dimensional hypercube $H_n$ is rainbow under the coordinate edge-colouring. Equivalently, every cycle of $H_n$ uses some coordinate direction at least twice.
\end{theorem}

A direct way to see this: traversing a cycle returns to its starting vertex, so along the cycle each coordinate must be flipped an even number of times; in particular any coordinate that changes at all changes at least twice, and hence the colour of the corresponding edges is repeated. This statement is the hypercube instance of the broader study of rainbow cycles in proper edge-colourings \cite{rainbowcycles}.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The coordinate colouring is defined on all unordered pairs of vertices and takes a fixed arbitrary coordinate as a junk value on pairs that are not edges; this is harmless, since only its values on edges enter the statement. A cycle is a closed walk that repeats no vertex except its endpoints, and it is rainbow when distinct edges of the cycle receive distinct colours.

