\section*{Problem 26: Full Cube Adjacency by Convex Bodies}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Sphere diagrams of convex bodies]
A \emph{convex body} in $\R^d$ is a compact convex set with nonempty interior; its boundary is a tamely embedded $(d-1)$-sphere. Let $K_1,\dots,K_n$ be convex bodies in $\R^d$. For $I\subseteq[n]$, the \emph{zone} $Z_I$ is the set of points that lie on none of the boundaries $\partial K_j$ and in exactly the bodies $K_j$ with $j\in I$. Two zones are \emph{adjacent} if their closures meet along a nonempty relatively open patch of one of the boundaries. Distinct boundaries may not share such a patch; tangencies, common intersection curves and points where several boundaries meet are allowed.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Realising the full cube]
The bodies $K_1,\dots,K_n$ \emph{realise} the cube $Q_n$ if no two boundaries share a patch, every one of the $2^n$ zones $Z_I$ is nonempty and connected, and the labelled region-adjacency graph, whose vertices are the zones and whose edges are the adjacent pairs, is exactly the hypercube graph $Q_n$ on the labels: $Z_I$ and $Z_J$ are adjacent precisely when $I$ and $J$ differ in one element.
\end{definition}

The model is the version with spheres of the three-dimensional Euler diagrams of Flower, Stapleton and Rodgers \cite{FlowerStapletonRodgers2014}. When the bodies are replaced by arbitrary tamely embedded spheres, the full cube $Q_n$ is realisable in $\R^2$ exactly for $n\le 3$, in $\R^3$ exactly for $n\le 4$, and in $\R^d$ for every $n$ when $d\ge 4$ (by spheres bounding star-shaped balls). The obstruction in $\R^3$ for $n=5$ comes from the theorem of Conway and Gordon \cite{ConwayGordon1983} that every spatial embedding of $K_6$ contains a pair of disjoint triangles with odd linking number. For round spheres the exact threshold is $n\le d+1$. For convex bodies, products of round-sphere diagrams in blocks of dimension two (and one block of dimension three when $d$ is odd) realise $Q_{\lfloor 3d/2\rfloor}$ in $\R^d$, which exceeds $d+1$ for $d\ge 4$. The theorem below shows that convex bodies, like tame spheres, realise arbitrarily large full cubes in every fixed dimension $d\ge 4$. These results are from unpublished notes \cite{SphereDiagramNotes2026} and have not yet been independently reviewed.

\begin{theorem}[Full cube adjacency by convex bodies \cite{SphereDiagramNotes2026}]
For every $d\ge 4$ and every $n\in\N$, the cube $Q_n$ is realised by $n$ convex bodies in $\R^d$.
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.}
The ambient space is $\R^d$ with the Euclidean topology, and $n=0$ (a single zone, the whole space) is included. A convex body is a compact convex set with nonempty interior. The condition that distinct boundaries share no patch is stated as: for $i\neq j$ and every open set $U$ meeting $\partial K_i$, the set $U\cap\partial K_i$ is not contained in $\partial K_j$. The adjacency between the labels $I$ and $I\cup\{i\}$, for $i\notin I$, is stated through a witness: a point of $\partial K_i$ that lies on no other boundary and in exactly the other bodies $K_j$ with $j\in I$. For convex bodies without shared patches this is equivalent to the definition through closures, and two zones whose labels differ in more than one element can never be adjacent; so the formal conditions say exactly that the region-adjacency graph is $Q_n$.

