\section*{Problem 034: Shrinkability of axis-aligned strips}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Range-capturing hypergraph]
A \emph{range family} in $\R^d$ is a collection $R$ of subsets of $\R^d$. A finite point set $V \subseteq \R^d$ is in \emph{general position} if any two distinct points of $V$ differ in every coordinate. For a range family $R$, a finite point set $V$ in general position and an integer $m \geq 0$, the \emph{$m$-uniform range-capturing hypergraph} $H\ev{V, R, m}$ has vertex set $V$, and a set $e \subseteq V$ is a hyperedge precisely when $|e| = m$ and $e = V \cap \rho$ for some range $\rho \in R$ (the points of $V$ lying in $\rho$ are exactly those of $e$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Shrinkable range family]
A range family $R$ is \emph{shrinkable} if for every finite point set $V$ in general position and every integer $m \geq 1$, each hyperedge of $H\ev{V, R, m}$ contains a hyperedge of $H\ev{V, R, m-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Axis-aligned strips]
For $d \in \N$, the family $R_{\mathrm{ST}}$ of \emph{axis-aligned strips} in $\R^d$ consists of all sets $\{x \in \R^d : a \leq x_i \leq b\}$ with a coordinate index $i \in \{0, \dots, d-1\}$ and $a, b \in \R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Shrinkability of strips]
For every $d \in \N$, the family $R_{\mathrm{ST}}$ of axis-aligned strips in $\R^d$ is shrinkable.
\end{theorem}

Planken and Ueckerdt \cite{plankenueckerdt} remark, without proof, that all range families considered in their paper except the translates of a convex polygon and unit disks are shrinkable. Shrinkability is the side condition of the reduction lemma of Keszegh and P\'alv\"olgyi \cite{keszeghpalvolgyi} (Problem~29), which together with the shallow hitting sets of Problem~31 gives a linear bound on the uniformity needed for polychromatic colourings of strip hypergraphs.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Points of $\R^d$ have coordinates $x_0, \dots, x_{d-1}$, the strip parameters $a, b$ are arbitrary reals (so a strip may be empty), and every quantification over point sets ranges over finite sets in general position. The hypergraph $H\ev{V, R, 0}$ is defined as well: its only possible hyperedge is the empty set, present exactly when some range of $R$ contains no point of $V$. The general-position assumption, made throughout \cite{plankenueckerdt}, cannot be dropped here: for the four corners of the unit square some strip captures all four points but none captures exactly three.

