\section*{Problem 37: Towards a Stallings-type theorem for finite groups (main theorem)}
\refstepcounter{section}

Graphs are simple, $r$ is a positive integer, and $r/2$ is rounded down where it bounds a length.

\begin{definition}[Cayley graphs and balls]
Let $\Gamma$ be a group and $S \subseteq \Gamma$ a generating set. The \emph{Cayley graph} $\mathrm{Cay}\ev{\Gamma, S}$ has vertex set $\Gamma$, two distinct elements $g, h$ being adjacent if $h = gs$ or $g = hs$ for some $s \in S$. For a vertex $v$ of a graph $G$, the \emph{ball} $B_r\ev{v, G}$ is the subgraph formed by the vertices and edges that lie on closed walks of length at most $r$ through $v$. For a vertex set $X$, $B_r\ev{v, G} - X$ is obtained from it by deleting the vertices in $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local separators]
A vertex $v$ of $G$ is an \emph{$r$-local cutvertex} if $B_r\ev{v, G} - v$ is disconnected, that is, two of its vertices are joined by no walk in $B_r\ev{v, G} - v$. For distinct vertices $v_0, v_1$, let $N\ev{v_0, v_1}$ be the set of vertices other than $v_0, v_1$ that are adjacent to $v_0$ or $v_1$. The \emph{connectivity graph} $C_r\ev{v_0, v_1, G}$ has vertex set $N\ev{v_0, v_1}$, two of its vertices being adjacent if for some $i \in \{0, 1\}$ they are joined by a walk in $B_r\ev{v_i, G} - v_0 - v_1$. The pair $\{v_0, v_1\}$ is an \emph{$r$-local $2$-separator} if $C_r\ev{v_0, v_1, G}$ is disconnected and $v_0, v_1$ have distance at most $r/2$ in $G$. An \emph{$r$-local separator of size at most two} is a set $\{v\}$ with $v$ an $r$-local cutvertex, or an $r$-local $2$-separator.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local coverings and ends]
A \emph{covering map} $p \colon H \to G$ is a surjective graph homomorphism that maps the closed neighbourhood of each vertex $x$ of $H$ bijectively onto the closed neighbourhood of $p\ev{x}$ in $G$. Two walks with the same end vertices are \emph{$r$-homotopic} if one arises from the other by finitely many steps, each inserting or deleting a closed subwalk of length at most $r$. For a connected graph $G$ and $r \geq 2$, the \emph{$r$-local covering} $p_r \colon G_r \to G$ is the covering whose characteristic subgroup is the subgroup $\pi_1^r\ev{G}$ of the fundamental group generated by the closed walks $W Q W^{-1}$ with $Q$ a closed walk once around a cycle of length at most $r$ \cite[\S 4]{DJKK}. Equivalently, up to equivalence of coverings, $p_r$ is the covering map $p \colon H \to G$ such that $H$ is connected, any two distinct vertices of $H$ with the same image have distance greater than $r$ (that is, closed walks of length at most $r$ in $G$ lift to closed walks), and every closed walk of $H$ is $r$-homotopic to the trivial walk. An \emph{end} of $H$ selects, for every finite vertex set $K$, a component of $H - K$, compatibly under inclusion of the sets $K$. A finite set $K$ \emph{separates} two ends if they select different components of $H - K$.
\end{definition}

For $k \geq 1$, $C_k$ denotes the cyclic group of order $k$.

\begin{theorem}[Carmesin--Kontogeorgiou--Kurkofka--Turner, Theorem 1 \cite{carmesinkontogeorgioukurkofkaturner}]
Let $\Gamma$ be a finite group that is nilpotent of class at most $n$, and let $r \geq \max\{4^{n+1}, 20\}$. Then the following assertions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the $r$-local covering of some Cayley graph of $\Gamma$ has two ends separated by a set of at most two vertices;
\item[(ii)] some Cayley graph of $\Gamma$ has an $r$-local separator of size at most two, and $|\Gamma| > r$;
\item[(iii)] $\Gamma \cong C_i \times C_j$ for some $i > r$ and $j \in \{1, 2\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Stallings' theorem characterises the finitely generated groups with at least two ends as those splitting over a finite subgroup; the theorem above is the low-order Stallings-type result for finite nilpotent groups proved in \cite{carmesinkontogeorgioukurkofkaturner}, where ends are replaced by local separators and local coverings of Cayley graphs. The paper notes that it does not extend to all finite groups (simple groups such as $A_5$ have Cayley graphs in which every vertex is a local cutvertex) and asks for extensions to solvable groups and to local separators of size at least three.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The ball defined through closed walks agrees with the distance-based ball of radius $r/2$ of \cite[Definition 2.3]{carmesinlocal2separators}: its vertices are those at distance at most $r/2$ from $v$, and its edges are those $xy$ with $d\ev{v, x} + d\ev{v, y} + 1 \leq r$. The definition of $r$-local $2$-separators is Definition 5.1 of \cite{carmesinkontogeorgioukurkofkaturner}. Mathlib has no fundamental group of a graph, so (i) uses the intrinsic description of $G_r$ in the definition above: the formal statement asks for a graph $H$ (on a type in the same universe as $\Gamma$) with a covering map $p$ onto the Cayley graph satisfying the three conditions, and for a set of at most two vertices of $H$ separating two ends of $H$. These conditions determine $p$ up to equivalence. The distance condition says that $p$ preserves $(r/2)$-balls \cite[Lemma 4.2]{DJKK}, so the characteristic subgroup of $p$ contains $\pi_1^r\ev{G}$ \cite[Lemma 4.4]{DJKK}. The last condition says $\pi_1\ev{H} = \pi_1^r\ev{H}$, and $p$ maps $\pi_1^r\ev{H}$ into $\pi_1^r\ev{G}$; so the characteristic subgroup is exactly $\pi_1^r\ev{G}$. Conversely, $G_r$ satisfies all three conditions (\cite[Lemma 4.3]{DJKK} and the proof of \cite[Lemma 4.6]{DJKK}). For $r \geq 2$, $r$-homotopy contains ordinary homotopy of walks (insertion of a backtrack $xyx$), and a closed walk is $r$-homotopic to the trivial walk if and only if its class lies in $\pi_1^r$; closed walks of length at most $r$ may be used in place of cycles, as they are generated by the short cycles they contain. Covers are connected, as in \cite{DJKK}. Ends are Mathlib's ends of a graph. A Cayley graph is Mathlib's \texttt{mulCayley} for an arbitrary generating set $S$, and $C_k$ is the multiplicative group of $\Z/k\Z$.

