\section*{Problem 42: The Riemann Hypothesis}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[The Riemann zeta function]
The \emph{Riemann zeta function} $\zeta$ is given for $\mathrm{Re}\, s > 1$ by $\zeta\ev{s} = \sum_{n \geq 1} n^{-s}$ and extends by analytic continuation to a meromorphic function on $\C$ whose only pole is a simple pole at $s = 1$. It vanishes at the \emph{trivial zeros} $s = -2, -4, -6, \dots$; every other zero is called \emph{nontrivial}.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[The Riemann Hypothesis \cite{riemann1859}]
Prove or disprove: every $s \in \C$ with $\zeta\ev{s} = 0$ that is not of the form $s = -2\ev{n+1}$ with $n \in \N$ satisfies $\mathrm{Re}\, s = \frac{1}{2}$.
\end{openproblem}

The hypothesis goes back to Riemann's 1859 memoir \cite{riemann1859} on the distribution of prime numbers and is one of the Clay Millennium Prize Problems; it remains open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The formal statement is Mathlib's \texttt{RiemannHypothesis}. Mathlib's \texttt{riemannZeta} is defined on all of $\C$, with the value $\ev{\gamma - \log 4\pi}/2 \neq 0$ at the pole $s = 1$; the statement also excludes $s = 1$ explicitly, which is harmless since this value is not zero.

