\section*{Problem 44: Whitney's uniqueness theorem}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Rotation systems]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph. A \emph{dart} of $G$ is an ordered pair $(u, v)$ of adjacent vertices; it is \emph{based} at $u$, and its \emph{reversal} is $\overline{(u, v)} = (v, u)$. A \emph{rotation system} of $G$ is a permutation $\rho$ of the set of darts that, for every vertex $v$, maps the darts based at $v$ to darts based at $v$ and permutes them in a single cycle; it thus fixes a cyclic order of the edges around each vertex. The \emph{reflection} of $\rho$ is the inverse permutation $\rho^{-1}$, which reverses every cyclic order.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Planar rotation systems]
The \emph{face permutation} of a rotation system $\rho$ is $d \mapsto \rho(\overline{d})$. The number $F(\rho)$ of \emph{faces} is the number of orbits of the face permutation plus the number of isolated vertices of $G$. The rotation system is \emph{planar} if
$$
|V(G)| - |E(G)| + F(\rho) = 2\,c(G),
$$
where $c(G)$ is the number of connected components of $G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$k$-connectivity]
A graph $G$ is \emph{$k$-connected} if it has more than $k$ vertices and $G - S$ is connected and nonempty for every set $S$ of fewer than $k$ vertices.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Whitney \cite{whitney1932}]\label{whitney}
Let $G$ be a finite $3$-connected graph and let $\rho_1, \rho_2$ be planar rotation systems of $G$. Then $\rho_2 = \rho_1$ or $\rho_2 = \rho_1^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}

By the Heffter--Edmonds correspondence, the rotation systems of $G$ describe the cellular embeddings of $G$ in closed oriented surfaces, one surface per component, and the planar ones are those embedding every component in the sphere. Theorem~\ref{whitney} is thus Whitney's result \cite{whitney1932} that a $3$-connected planar graph has a unique embedding in the sphere up to reflection. The hypothesis cannot be weakened to $2$-connectivity: $K_{2,4}$ has six planar rotation systems.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Planarity is defined combinatorially, through the Euler characteristic of a rotation system, rather than topologically. Isolated vertices are counted as faces, so that the Euler bound reads $2$ per component. No separate planarity hypothesis on $G$ is needed, since it follows from the existence of $\rho_1$.

