\section*{Problem 45: Rainbow cycles in proper edge-colourings (Theorem 1.1)}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Proper edge-colourings and rainbow cycles]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph. An \emph{edge-colouring} of $G$ assigns to every edge a colour from an arbitrary set of colours; it is \emph{proper} if any two distinct edges sharing an endpoint receive different colours. A cycle of $G$ is \emph{rainbow} if its edges have pairwise distinct colours. The \emph{average degree} of $G$ is $d(G) = 2|E(G)|/|V(G)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Alon--Buci\'c--Sauermann--Zakharov--Zamir, Theorem 1.1 \cite{rainbowcyclesessentiallytight}]\label{rainbowmain}
There is an absolute constant $C > 0$ such that every properly edge-coloured graph $G$ on $n \geq 3$ vertices with $d(G) \geq C \log n \, \log\log n$ contains a rainbow cycle.
\end{theorem}

This is the main theorem of \cite{rainbowcyclesessentiallytight}. It is tight up to the factor $\log\log n$: the $d$-dimensional hypercube, with each edge coloured by the coordinate in which its endpoints differ, has $n = 2^d$ vertices and average degree $d = \log_2 n$, and contains no rainbow cycle (Problem 14).

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Logarithms are natural; the base only affects the constant $C$. A cycle is a closed walk with at least one edge, no repeated edges and no repeated vertices other than its endpoints, and it is rainbow when the list of colours along it has no repetitions. The theorem is stated as the instance $f(n) = \log n \, \log\log n$ of a predicate asserting that, for some $C > 0$, average degree at least $C f(n)$ forces a rainbow cycle in every properly edge-coloured graph on $n \geq 3$ vertices.

