\section*{Problem 51: Helly number for agreeing orders of marked cliques (Bir\'o--Lehel--T\'oth, Theorem 1)}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Marked cliques and agreeing orders]
Let $r \ge 3$ and let $V$ be a finite set. The \emph{$r$-uniform clique} on $V$ has every $r$-element subset of $V$ as an edge. It is \emph{2-extreme marked} if every edge $e$ carries a set $\partial e \subseteq e$ of two \emph{boundary} vertices. For $U \subseteq V$, a linear order $L$ on $U$ is an \emph{agreeing linear order of $U$} if for every edge $e \subseteq U$ the $L$-minimum and the $L$-maximum of $e$ are exactly the two vertices of $\partial e$, that is, every vertex of $e$ lies between the two boundary vertices. The marked clique $H$ has an \emph{agreeing linear order} if $V$ has one.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bir\'o--Lehel--T\'oth, Theorem 1 \cite{birolehltothhelly}]
Let $r \ge 3$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Let $H$ be a 2-extreme marked $r$-uniform clique on at least $2r-2$ vertices. Then $H$ has an agreeing linear order if and only if every set of $2r-2$ vertices has an agreeing linear order.
\item[(ii)] The number $2r-2$ cannot be lowered: there is a 2-extreme marked $r$-uniform clique on $2r-2$ vertices in which every set of $2r-3$ vertices has an agreeing linear order, but which has no agreeing linear order.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The case $r = 3$ is of particular interest in the axiomatic theory of betweenness. The paper \cite{birolehltothhelly} also proves Helly-type theorems for other rules of marking, and shows that for one of them no Helly-type statement holds.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} A linear order on $U$ is encoded by a function $V \to \mathbb{N}$ that is injective on $U$; its values outside $U$ play no role. Part (ii) is stated with exactly $2r-2$ vertices.

