\section*{Problem 52: Distinct clique sizes in uniform hypergraphs (Gao, Theorem 1.1; Erd\H{o}s problem \#775)}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Cliques in uniform hypergraphs]
Let $k \ge 2$ and let $H$ be a \emph{$k$-uniform hypergraph} on a finite vertex set $V$, that is, a set of $k$-element subsets of $V$ (its \emph{edges}). A set $S \subseteq V$ is \emph{complete} if every $k$-element subset of $S$ is an edge of $H$. A \emph{clique} of $H$ is a complete set that is not properly contained in another complete set.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gao, Theorem 1.1 \cite{gaocliqueshypergraphs}]
For all integers $k \ge 3$ and $C \ge 0$ there is $N = N(k,C)$ such that for every $n \ge N$, every $k$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices has at most $n - C$ different sizes of cliques.
\end{theorem}

Equivalently, the largest number $g(n,k)$ of distinct clique sizes in a $k$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices is $n - \omega(1)$. For graphs, Moon and Moser \cite{moonmosercliques} proved $n - \log_2 n - 2\log_2\log_2 n < g(n,2) \le n - \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor$. Erd\H{o}s constructed a $3$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices with cliques of $n - \log^* n$ distinct sizes and asked whether $n - C$ distinct sizes are impossible for some constant $C$ and all large $n$ (Erd\H{o}s problem \#775 \cite{bloomerdos775}); the case $k = 3$ of the theorem answers this question.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The vertex set is $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$, and every element of it is a vertex of $H$. Sets with fewer than $k$ vertices are complete vacuously, so a maximal $(k-1)$-element set contained in no edge counts as a clique; compared with a convention in which cliques must contain an edge, this changes the number of sizes by at most one, which is immaterial since $C$ is arbitrary. The bound $n - C$ is computed in natural numbers.

