\section*{Problem 54: Cycle Double Cover Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Multigraphs, bridges and cycles]
A finite \emph{multigraph} $G$ consists of a finite vertex set $V$, a finite edge set $E$, and for each edge an unordered pair of endpoints; loops (equal endpoints) and parallel edges are allowed. An edge with endpoints $x, y$ is a \emph{bridge} if $x$ and $y$ are not connected in $G - e$, and $G$ is \emph{bridgeless} if it has no bridge. A set $F \subseteq E$ is \emph{even} if every vertex is incident with an even number of non-loop edges of $F$. A \emph{cycle} is a nonempty inclusion-minimal even edge set; thus a loop is a one-edge cycle and two parallel edges form a two-edge cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cycle double cover]
A \emph{cycle double cover} of $G$ is a finite list of cycles of $G$, repetitions allowed, in which every edge of $G$ lies in exactly two entries of the list.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cycle double cover conjecture \cite{szekerespolyhedral,seymoursums}]
Every finite bridgeless multigraph has a cycle double cover.
\end{theorem}

The statement was conjectured by Szekeres \cite{szekerespolyhedral} and independently by Seymour \cite{seymoursums}, and it was a central open problem in graph theory for five decades. In July 2026 OpenAI announced a proof; an exposition of this proof, with slight modifications, has been given by Oum \cite{oumcdcexposition}. The proof has not yet been refereed, and the problem is labelled Research on the strength of it.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Each edge has an unordered pair of endpoints, so loops and parallel edges are allowed; a loop contributes nothing to the parity condition, being incident twice with its vertex. A bridge is defined through connectivity of the underlying simple graph of $G - e$, so a loop is never a bridge and an edge with a parallel copy is not a bridge. A cycle double cover is a list of cycles, so the same cycle may occur twice; only the number of occurrences of each edge matters, not the order of the list. The vertex set only needs to be finite, and isolated vertices are allowed.

