\section*{Problem 55: Exponentially Many Circuit Double Covers}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Circuits and circuit double covers]
Let $G$ be a finite graph. A set $F \subseteq E\ev{G}$ of edges is \emph{even} if every vertex of $G$ is incident with an even number of edges of $F$. A \emph{circuit} of $G$ is a nonempty inclusion-minimal even edge set; in a simple graph these are exactly the edge sets of cycles. A \emph{circuit double cover} of $G$ is a finite multiset of circuits such that every edge of $G$ lies in exactly two of its members, counted with multiplicity. Two circuit double covers are equal when they are equal as multisets, so the order of the circuits does not matter.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Connectivity and edge-colourings]
A graph $G$ is \emph{$k$-connected} if it has more than $k$ vertices and $G - S$ is connected for every set $S$ of fewer than $k$ vertices. It is \emph{cubic} if every vertex has degree $3$. A \emph{proper $3$-edge-colouring} of $G$ is a map $c \colon E\ev{G} \to \{1, 2, 3\}$ that gives distinct colours to any two edges sharing a vertex.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hu\v{s}ek--\v{S}\'amal]
Let $G$ be a simple $2$-connected cubic graph on $n$ vertices that has a proper $3$-edge-colouring. Then $G$ has at least $2^{n/2 - 1}$ circuit double covers.
\end{theorem}

This is Theorem~1.7 of \cite{husekSamal2026}. It confirms, for $3$-edge-colourable graphs, the conjecture of the same authors that every $2$-connected cubic graph on $n$ vertices has at least $2^{n/2-1}$ circuit double covers; the bound is attained by the Klee graphs.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The graph is a finite multigraph whose edges are indexed by a finite type, assumed to be simple (no loops, no two edges with the same pair of ends); cubicity and $2$-connectivity are stated for its underlying simple graph. A circuit double cover is recorded as a function assigning multiplicity $0$, $1$ or $2$ to every circuit such that every edge has total multiplicity exactly $2$; counting these functions counts multisets of circuits, and in a cubic graph no circuit is ever used twice, so this agrees with the count of \cite{husekSamal2026}. The exponent $n/2 - 1$ is computed in natural numbers, which is exact here because a cubic graph has an even number $n \geq 4$ of vertices. The proper $3$-edge-colouring is a hypothesis given as data; only its existence matters.

