\section*{Problem 56: Exponentially Many Circuit Double Covers at High Girth}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Circuits, girth and circuit double covers]
Let $G$ be a finite graph. A set $F \subseteq E\ev{G}$ of edges is \emph{even} if every vertex of $G$ is incident with an even number of edges of $F$. A \emph{circuit} of $G$ is a nonempty inclusion-minimal even edge set; in a simple graph these are exactly the edge sets of cycles. The graph $G$ has \emph{girth at least $g$} if every circuit has at least $g$ edges. A \emph{circuit double cover} of $G$ is a finite multiset of circuits such that every edge of $G$ lies in exactly two of its members, counted with multiplicity; the order of the circuits does not matter.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Connectivity]
A graph $G$ is \emph{$k$-connected} if it has more than $k$ vertices and $G - S$ is connected for every set $S$ of fewer than $k$ vertices. It is \emph{cubic} if every vertex has degree $3$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hu\v{s}ek--\v{S}\'amal]
There exist a real number $c > 0$ and an integer $n_0$ such that every simple $3$-connected cubic graph of girth at least $16$ on $n \geq n_0$ vertices has at least $2^{cn}$ circuit double covers.
\end{theorem}

This is Theorem~1.8 of \cite{husekSamal2026}, stated there as: the number of circuit double covers of a $3$-connected cubic graph on $n$ vertices with girth at least $16$ is $2^{\Omega\ev{n}}$. Unlike Problem~55, no $3$-edge-colourability is assumed, so the theorem also covers snarks of large girth.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The graph is a finite multigraph whose edges are indexed by a finite type, assumed to be simple; cubicity and $3$-connectivity are stated for its underlying simple graph. The bound $2^{\Omega\ev{n}}$ is written out with explicit constants: a single pair $\ev{c, n_0}$ must work for all graphs, and $2^{cn}$ is a real power. Circuit double covers are counted as in Problem~55, as functions assigning multiplicity $0$, $1$ or $2$ to every circuit with every edge of total multiplicity exactly $2$, that is, as multisets of circuits.

