\section*{Problem 57: Divisorial Gonality of Finite Graphs}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Divisors on a graph]
Let $G$ be a finite connected multigraph without loops, with vertex set $V$ and $\lvert E\rvert$ edges, and let $g = \lvert E\rvert - \lvert V\rvert + 1$ be its \emph{genus} (cycle rank). A \emph{divisor} on $G$ is a function $D \colon V \to \Z$; its \emph{degree} is $\deg D = \sum_{v \in V} D\ev{v}$, and $D$ is \emph{effective} if $D\ev{v} \geq 0$ for every $v$. \emph{Firing} a vertex $v$ sends one chip along each edge at $v$: it lowers $D\ev{v}$ by the degree of $v$ and raises $D\ev{w}$ by the number of edges between $v$ and $w$, for every $w \neq v$. Two divisors $D$ and $D'$ are \emph{linearly equivalent}, written $D \sim D'$, if $D' - D$ lies in the subgroup of $\Z^V$ generated by these firing vectors.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rank at least one]
A divisor $D$ on $G$ has \emph{rank at least one} if for every vertex $q$ there is an effective divisor $E \sim D$ with $E\ev{q} \geq 1$; that is, firings can move the chips of $D$ so that $q$ holds a chip and no vertex is in debt.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Baker's gonality conjecture]
Prove or disprove: every finite connected loopless multigraph of genus $g$ has an effective divisor $D$ with $\deg D \leq \lfloor \ev{g+3}/2 \rfloor$ and rank at least one.
\end{openproblem}

This is Conjecture~3.10 of \cite{Baker2008Specialization}, the case $r = 1$ of the Brill--Noether existence conjecture for graphs (Problem~58) and the graph analogue of the classical bound $\lfloor \ev{g+3}/2 \rfloor$ on the gonality of an algebraic curve of genus $g$. Atanasov and Ranganathan proved it for $g \leq 5$ \cite{AtanasovRanganathan2018LowGenus}; it is open in general. The analogous statement for metric graphs is a theorem of Baker (see Problem~59).

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The definitions follow the Lean chip-firing development of Mavani and Pflueger \cite{MavaniPflueger2026ChipFiringLean}, rebased on the multigraph type of Problems~54--56: edges are indexed by a finite type (so parallel edges are allowed), loops are excluded, there is at least one vertex, and connectivity is that of the underlying simple graph. The genus is computed in $\Z$. The bound $\lfloor \ev{g+3}/2 \rfloor$ is integer division. Rank at least one is stated directly in the chip-moving form above, which is equivalent to the usual condition that $D - q$ is linearly equivalent to an effective divisor for every vertex $q$.

