\section*{Problem 60: Chv\'atal's Toughness Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Toughness]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph and, for $S \subseteq V\ev{G}$, let $c\ev{G - S}$ be the number of connected components of the graph obtained by deleting $S$. For a real number $t$, the graph $G$ is \emph{$t$-tough} if
$$
t \cdot c\ev{G - S} \ \leq\ \lvert S\rvert \qquad \text{for every } S \subseteq V\ev{G} \text{ with } c\ev{G - S} \geq 2.
$$
The graph $G$ is \emph{Hamiltonian} if it has a cycle through every vertex exactly once.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Chv\'atal's toughness conjecture]
Prove or disprove: there is a real number $t > 0$ such that every $t$-tough graph on at least three vertices is Hamiltonian.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture is due to Chv\'atal \cite{chvatal1973tough}, who introduced toughness and observed that every Hamiltonian graph is $1$-tough. Bauer, Broersma and Veldman \cite{bauerbroersmaveldman2000} constructed $\ev{9/4 - \varepsilon}$-tough non-Hamiltonian graphs for every $\varepsilon > 0$, so not every $2$-tough graph is Hamiltonian and any valid $t$ is at least $9/4$. The conjecture is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} With $S = \emptyset$, a disconnected graph is not $t$-tough for any $t > 0$, so $t$-tough graphs are connected. A complete graph has no set $S$ with $c\ev{G - S} \geq 2$ and is therefore $t$-tough for every $t$, matching the usual convention that its toughness is infinite. A disproof must, for every $t > 0$, exhibit a $t$-tough non-Hamiltonian graph on at least three vertices.

