\section*{Problem 61: Seymour's Exact Edge-Colouring Conjecture for Planar Multigraphs}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Oddly edge-connected and edge-colourable multigraphs]
Let $G$ be a finite loopless multigraph, so parallel edges are allowed. The \emph{degree} of a vertex is the number of edges incident with it, counted with multiplicity, and $G$ is \emph{$d$-regular} if every vertex has degree $d$. For $X \subseteq V\ev{G}$ let $\delta\ev{X}$ be the set of edges with one end in $X$ and the other in $V\ev{G} \setminus X$. The multigraph $G$ is \emph{oddly $d$-edge-connected} if $\lvert \delta\ev{X} \rvert \geq d$ for every set $X \subseteq V\ev{G}$ of odd size. It is \emph{$d$-edge-colourable} if there is a map $c \colon E\ev{G} \to \{1, \dots, d\}$ giving distinct colours to any two edges with a common end. It is \emph{planar} if its underlying simple graph, obtained by merging parallel edges, is planar.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Seymour's exact edge-colouring conjecture]
Prove or disprove: for every integer $d \geq 0$, every $d$-regular, oddly $d$-edge-connected, planar loopless multigraph is $d$-edge-colourable.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture was proposed by Seymour in about 1973 and appears in \cite{seymour1979multicolourings}. The condition is necessary, since every colour class of a $d$-edge-colouring of a $d$-regular multigraph is a perfect matching and so meets $\delta\ev{X}$ for every odd set $X$; without planarity it is not sufficient, as the Petersen graph is $3$-regular and oddly $3$-edge-connected but not $3$-edge-colourable. For $d = 3$ the conjecture is equivalent, by Tait's work, to the Four Colour Theorem. The cases $d = 4, 5$ are due to Guenin \cite{guenin2003planar}, the case $d = 6$ to Dvo\v{r}\'ak, Kawarabayashi and Kr\'al' \cite{dvorakkawarabayashikral2016six}, the case $d = 7$ to Edwards and Kawarabayashi, with a simpler proof by Chudnovsky, Edwards, Kawarabayashi and Seymour \cite{chudnovskyedwardskawarabayashiseymour7}, and the case $d = 8$ to Chudnovsky, Edwards and Seymour \cite{chudnovskyedwardsseymour8}. The conjecture is open for every $d \geq 9$.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} A multigraph is given by a finite set of vertices, a finite set of edges and a map sending each edge to an unordered pair of ends; loops are excluded by hypothesis, and degrees and $\lvert \delta\ev{X} \rvert$ count edges with multiplicity. The condition ``oddly $d$-edge-connected'' quantifies over every vertex set $X$ of odd size, including $X = V\ev{G}$ itself; since $\delta\ev{V\ev{G}} = \emptyset$, for $d \geq 1$ it forces $\lvert V\ev{G} \rvert$ to be even, exactly as in the standard definition \cite{chudnovskyedwardsseymour8}. Planarity of the underlying simple graph is expressed combinatorially: some rotation system (a cyclic order of the edge-ends at every vertex) has Euler characteristic $2c$, where $c$ is the number of connected components, that is, it embeds every component in the sphere. Disconnected multigraphs are allowed.

