\section*{Problem 63: Mader's Connectivity Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Average degree and vertex connectivity]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph on $n$ vertices. Its \emph{average degree} is
$$
\overline{d}\ev{G} \ :=\ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{v \in V\ev{G}} \deg\ev{v} \ =\ \frac{2\lvert E\ev{G} \rvert}{n},
$$
with $\overline{d}\ev{G} := 0$ when $n = 0$. For an integer $\ell \geq 0$, a graph is \emph{$\ell$-connected} if it has more than $\ell$ vertices and stays connected after the deletion of any set of fewer than $\ell$ vertices.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Mader's connectivity conjecture]
Prove or disprove: for every integer $k \geq 1$, every finite simple graph of average degree at least $3k - 1$ contains a $\ev{k+1}$-connected subgraph.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture is due to Mader \cite{mader1979survey}, who had proved in 1972 that average degree at least $4k - 2$ suffices \cite{mader1972existenz}; the linear factor has since been improved, but not to $3$. Krone \cite{krone2025minimum} proved the minimum-degree analogue: every graph of minimum degree at least $3k - 1$ contains a $\ev{k+1}$-connected subgraph on more than $2k$ vertices. If the conjecture is true, the bound $3k - 1$ is sharp for every $k$ \cite{krone2025minimum}. The conjecture is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The average degree is a rational number, and the convention $\overline{d}\ev{G} = 0$ for the empty graph is harmless, as the hypothesis then fails for $k \geq 1$. The subgraph is required to be an induced subgraph $G[S]$ for some vertex set $S$; this loses nothing, since adding edges preserves vertex connectivity. The restriction $k \geq 1$ is needed: for $k = 0$ the statement would ask every graph, including the edgeless ones, for a connected subgraph on at least two vertices.

