\section*{Problem 64: The Berge--Fulkerson Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Cubic multigraphs and perfect matchings]
A multigraph is \emph{cubic} if it has no loops and every vertex is incident with exactly three edges, counted with multiplicity; parallel edges are allowed. A \emph{perfect matching} is a set $M$ of edges such that every vertex is incident with exactly one edge of $M$. An edge is a \emph{bridge} if deleting it leaves its two ends in different connected components, and a multigraph is \emph{bridgeless} if it has no bridge.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[The Berge--Fulkerson conjecture]
Prove or disprove: every bridgeless cubic multigraph has six perfect matchings $M_1, \dots, M_6$, not necessarily distinct, such that every edge lies in exactly two of them.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture was published by Fulkerson \cite{fulkerson1971blocking} and is also attributed to Berge. Equivalently, the multigraph obtained by doubling every edge is $6$-edge-colourable. For a $3$-edge-colourable cubic graph the three colour classes, each taken twice, are such matchings, so a counterexample would be a snark (a bridgeless cubic graph that is not $3$-edge-colourable); the Petersen graph satisfies the conjecture. By a theorem of Mazzuoccolo \cite{mazzuoccolo2011equivalence}, the conjecture is equivalent to Berge's conjecture that every bridgeless cubic graph has five perfect matchings whose union is the whole edge set. It implies the Fan--Raspaud conjecture \cite{fanraspaud1994} that every bridgeless cubic graph has three perfect matchings with no edge common to all three. The conjecture is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The six matchings are indexed by $\{1, \dots, 6\}$, repetitions are allowed, and ``exactly two'' counts the indices $i$ with $e \in M_i$. Degrees count only non-loop edges, so the loopless hypothesis makes ``every vertex has degree $3$'' the usual notion of a cubic multigraph. The edge set is finite; the vertex set is not assumed finite, but $3$-regularity forces it. Bridges are as defined above, so an edge with a parallel copy is never a bridge.

