\section*{Problem 65: The Kahn--Kalai Counterexample to Borsuk's Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Diameter and Borsuk's conjecture]
For a bounded set $X \subseteq \R^d$ let $\operatorname{diam} X := \sup\{\lVert x - y \rVert : x, y \in X\}$, where $\lVert \cdot \rVert$ is the Euclidean norm. Borsuk's conjecture \cite{borsuk1933drei} asserts that every bounded set $X \subseteq \R^d$ can be covered by $d + 1$ sets of diameter strictly smaller than $\operatorname{diam} X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kahn--Kalai]
There is a finite set $S \subseteq \R^{1325}$ with $\operatorname{diam} S > 0$ such that for every map $c \colon S \to \{1, \dots, 1326\}$ there are $x, y \in S$ with $c\ev{x} = c\ev{y}$ and $\lVert x - y \rVert = \operatorname{diam} S$. In particular, $S$ cannot be covered by $1326$ sets of smaller diameter, so Borsuk's conjecture fails in dimension $1325$.
\end{theorem}

Kahn and Kalai \cite{KahnKalai1993Borsuk} disproved Borsuk's conjecture in 1993, using the Frankl--Wilson intersection theorem \cite{FranklWilson1981Intersection}; Jenrich \cite{Jenrich2018KahnKalai} gives a detailed and corrected account of their counterexample in dimension $1325$.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The set lives in $\R^{1325}$ with the Euclidean distance. Being finite, it is bounded and its diameter is attained. For a finite set, a cover by $1326$ sets of smaller diameter is the same as a colouring with $1326$ colours without a monochromatic pair at distance $\operatorname{diam} S$, the colour classes being the parts; colourings need not use every colour, so covers by fewer sets are excluded as well. The condition $\operatorname{diam} S > 0$ rules out one-point sets and makes the two points of the monochromatic pair distinct.

