\section*{Problem 66: The Tangle-Decider Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Separations and tangles]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph with vertex set $V$. A \emph{separation} of $G$ is an ordered pair $\ev{A, B}$ of subsets of $V$ with $A \cup B = V$ such that no edge joins $A \setminus B$ to $B \setminus A$; its \emph{order} is $\lvert A \cap B \rvert$, and $\ev{B, A}$ is its \emph{inverse}. For an integer $k \geq 0$, a \emph{tangle of order $k$} is a set $\tau$ of separations of order less than $k$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] for every separation of order less than $k$, exactly one of it and its inverse lies in $\tau$;
\item[(ii)] there are no $\ev{A_1, B_1}, \ev{A_2, B_2}, \ev{A_3, B_3} \in \tau$, not necessarily distinct, with $G[A_1] \cup G[A_2] \cup G[A_3] = G$, that is, such that every vertex and every edge of $G$ lies in some $G[A_i]$.
\end{enumerate}
A set $X \subseteq V$ \emph{decides} $\tau$ (or induces $\tau$ by majority vote) if $\lvert A \cap X \rvert < \lvert B \cap X \rvert$ for every $\ev{A, B} \in \tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[The tangle-decider conjecture]
Prove or disprove: for every finite simple graph $G$ and every $k \geq 0$, every tangle of order $k$ in $G$ is decided by some set of vertices of $G$.
\end{openproblem}

The question was raised by Diestel, Hundertmark and Lemanczyk \cite{DiestelHundertmarkLemanczyk2019Profiles}. Elbracht, Kneip and Teegen \cite{ElbrachtKneipTeegen2020Weighted} proved the weighted version: for every tangle $\tau$ of order $k$ there is a weight function $w \colon V \to \N$ such that a separation $\ev{A, B}$ of order less than $k$ lies in $\tau$ if and only if $w\ev{A} < w\ev{B}$. The answer is positive for $k \leq 3$ and open for every $k \geq 4$; Albrechtsen, von Bergen, Jacobs, Knappe and Wollan \cite{AlbrechtsenEtAl2024VertexSets} showed that, for each fixed $k$, a counterexample of order $k$ would yield one on a connected graph whose size is bounded by a function of $k$ alone.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} A tangle is recorded as the set of its chosen separations $\ev{A, B}$, with the side $A$ that appears in condition (ii) written first. Condition (ii) requires the three induced subgraphs to cover all vertices, isolated ones included, and all edges. The separation $\ev{V, V}$ is its own inverse, so condition (i) shows that a tangle of order $k$ can exist only if $k \leq \lvert V \rvert$. Deciding is stated in one direction only; it implies the two-sided form ``$\ev{A, B} \in \tau$ if and only if $\lvert A \cap X \rvert < \lvert B \cap X \rvert$'' for separations of order less than $k$, because the inverse of a separation not in $\tau$ lies in $\tau$.

