\section*{Problem 68: Negami's Planar Cover Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Graph covers]
Let $G$ and $H$ be simple graphs. A \emph{cover} of $G$ by $H$ is a surjective graph homomorphism $p \colon V\ev{H} \to V\ev{G}$ such that for every vertex $w$ of $H$ the map $p$ restricts to a bijection from the neighbourhood of $w$ in $H$ onto the neighbourhood of $p\ev{w}$ in $G$. The graph $G$ has a \emph{finite planar cover} if it is covered by some finite planar graph $H$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Planarity via rotation systems]
A \emph{rotation system} on a finite simple graph $H$ is a permutation of its darts (oriented edges) that cycles through the darts leaving each vertex in a single cycle; it records a cyclic order of the edge-ends around every vertex. Its \emph{faces} are the orbits of the permutation ``reverse the dart, then rotate'', together with one face for each isolated vertex. The graph $H$ is \emph{planar} (of genus zero) if it has a rotation system with $|V\ev{H}| - |E\ev{H}| + \#\text{faces} = 2c$, where $c$ is the number of connected components of $H$.
\end{definition}

The graph $K_{1,2,2,2}$ is the complete multipartite graph with parts of sizes $1, 2, 2, 2$: the octahedron $K_{2,2,2}$ together with an apex vertex adjacent to all six of its vertices.

\begin{openproblem}[Negami's planar cover conjecture, remaining case]
Prove or disprove: $K_{1,2,2,2}$ has no finite planar cover.
\end{openproblem}

Negami conjectured that a connected graph has a finite planar cover if and only if it embeds in the projective plane. Graphs embeddable in the projective plane have finite planar covers, and the work surveyed by Hlin\v{e}n\'y \cite{Hlineny2010Negami} reduces the remaining direction to the single graph $K_{1,2,2,2}$: the conjecture holds if and only if $K_{1,2,2,2}$ has no finite planar cover. Annor, Nikolayevsky and Payne showed that $K_{1,2,2,2}$ has no $n$-fold planar cover for $n < 14$ \cite{AnnorEtAl2023SmallCovers} and proved further restrictions on a possible planar cover \cite{AnnorEtAl2024Negami}; the general statement is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Covers are defined directly, as above; the covering graph $H$ is any finite simple graph, need not be connected, and the number of sheets is not bounded. Planarity of $H$ is the combinatorial notion via rotation systems given above, which by the Heffter--Edmonds principle is equivalent to topological planarity for finite graphs.

