\section*{Problem 70: Independence of the Continuum Hypothesis}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[First-order ZFC]
The language of set theory is the first-order language with equality and a single binary relation symbol $\in$, and no function symbols. The theory $\mathrm{ZFC}$ consists of the axioms of extensionality, empty set, pairing, union and power set; infinity (some set contains an empty set and contains $x \cup \{x\}$ whenever it contains $x$); foundation (every nonempty set has an $\in$-minimal element); choice (every family of nonempty, pairwise disjoint sets has a set meeting each member in exactly one element); and, for every formula $\varphi$ with any number of parameters, the separation axiom $\forall \bar p\, \forall A\, \exists S\, \forall z\, \ev{z \in S \leftrightarrow z \in A \wedge \varphi\ev{\bar p, z}}$ and the replacement axiom $\forall \bar p\, \forall A\, \ew{\ev{\forall x \in A\, \exists! y\, \varphi\ev{\bar p, x, y}} \to \exists B\, \forall x \in A\, \exists y \in B\, \varphi\ev{\bar p, x, y}}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The Continuum Hypothesis]
Let $\omega$ be the $\subseteq$-least set that contains an empty set and is closed under $x \mapsto x \cup \{x\}$, and let $P\ev{\omega}$ be its power set. Write $X \preceq Y$ if there is an injection from $X$ into $Y$, coded as a set of Kuratowski ordered pairs $\{\{a\}, \{a, b\}\}$. The sentence $\mathrm{CH}$ says: for every set $X$ with $X \preceq P\ev{\omega}$, either $X \preceq \omega$ or $P\ev{\omega} \preceq X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Independence]
A sentence $\varphi$ is a \emph{semantic consequence} of a theory $T$, written $T \models \varphi$, if $\varphi$ holds in every model of $T$. It is \emph{independent} of $T$ if neither $T \models \varphi$ nor $T \models \neg\varphi$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[G\"odel, Cohen]
$\mathrm{CH}$ is independent of $\mathrm{ZFC}$.
\end{theorem}

G\"odel \cite{godel1938consistency} built a model of $\mathrm{ZFC} + \mathrm{CH}$ (the constructible universe) and Cohen \cite{cohen1963independence} a model of $\mathrm{ZFC} + \neg\mathrm{CH}$ (by forcing). The Flypitch project of Han and van Doorn \cite{hanvandoorn2020flypitch} formalised this independence result in Lean~3.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The language, the axioms and $\mathrm{CH}$ are spelled out as sentences of Mathlib's first-order logic. Consequence is semantic because Mathlib has no proof calculus for first-order logic; by G\"odel's completeness theorem it agrees with provability. Over $\mathrm{ZFC}$ the form of $\mathrm{CH}$ used here is equivalent to $2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_1$, by comparability of cardinals and the Cantor--Schr\"oder--Bernstein theorem. The statement has no hypothesis that $\mathrm{ZFC}$ is consistent: Lean's type theory, with its universes, is strong enough to provide models of $\mathrm{ZFC}$. The problem file also states the alternatives $\mathrm{ZFC} \models \mathrm{CH}$ and $\mathrm{ZFC} \models \neg\mathrm{CH}$, which are not the target; by the theorem above, a proof of either in Lean would show Lean inconsistent.

