\section*{Problem 72: The Anonymous Lower Bound for Superpermutations}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Superpermutation]
Let $n \ge 0$ and let $[n] = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$. A \emph{superpermutation} on $n$ symbols is a finite word $w$ over the alphabet $[n]$ such that for every permutation $\sigma$ of $[n]$ the word $\sigma\ev{0}\,\sigma\ev{1} \cdots \sigma\ev{n-1}$ occurs in $w$ as a contiguous factor (a block of consecutive letters).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Anonymous; Houston--Pantone--Vatter]
For every $n \ge 2$, every superpermutation on $n$ symbols has length at least
$$
n! + \ev{n-1}! + \ev{n-2}! + n - 3.
$$
\end{theorem}

The bound was proved in 2011 by an anonymous poster on 4chan and written up by Houston, Pantone and Vatter \cite{houstonpantonevatter2018lowerbound}. It improves the bound $n! + \ev{n-1}! + n - 2$ of Ashlock and Tillotson \cite{ashlocktillotson1993}. It is tight for $2 \le n \le 4$, where the minimal lengths are $3, 9, 33$, and is one less than the minimal length $153$ for $n = 5$; see the survey of Engen and Vatter \cite{engenvatter2021containing}.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The formal statement has no hypothesis on $n$. It uses natural-number arithmetic, in which $a - b = 0$ for $a < b$, so the bound reads $1 + 1 + 1 + 0 - 3 = 0$ for $n = 0$ and $1 + 1 + 1 + 1 - 3 = 1$ for $n = 1$ (the minimal length for $n = 1$).

