\section*{Problem 073: The Alon--Tarsi Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Latin squares and their sign]
A \emph{Latin square} of order $n$ is an $n \times n$ array $L = (L_{ij})$, with rows and columns indexed by $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$ and entries in $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$, in which no symbol is repeated in any row or column. Each row $i$ then defines a permutation $j \mapsto L_{ij}$ and each column $j$ a permutation $i \mapsto L_{ij}$ of $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$. The \emph{sign} of $L$ is the product of the signs of these $2n$ permutations; $L$ is \emph{even} if its sign is $+1$ and \emph{odd} if it is $-1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Alon--Tarsi conjecture]\label{alontarsi}
Prove or disprove: for every even $n$, the number of even Latin squares of order $n$ differs from the number of odd Latin squares of order $n$.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture is due to Alon and Tarsi \cite{alontarsi1992colorings}. For odd $n \geq 3$ the two numbers are equal, since exchanging two rows of a Latin square changes its sign; this is why only even orders are considered. Drisko \cite{drisko1997} proved the conjecture for $n = p + 1$ and Glynn \cite{glynn2010} for $n = p - 1$, where $p$ is an odd prime; hence it holds for every even $n \leq 24$, and the smallest open case is $n = 26$. Huang and Rota \cite{huangrota1994} showed that the conjecture for a given even $n$ implies Rota's basis conjecture in dimension $n$ over fields of characteristic zero, and through the polynomial method of \cite{alontarsi1992colorings} it implies the Dinitz conjecture for even $n$.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The formal statement counts all Latin squares with symbols $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$. Relabelling the symbols does not change the sign, so the numbers of even and of odd Latin squares are $n!$ times the corresponding numbers of \emph{reduced} Latin squares (first row $0, 1, \dots, n-1$), with which the conjecture is often stated; the two formulations are equivalent. The case $n = 0$ is included and holds trivially: the empty array is the unique Latin square of order $0$, and it is even.

