\section*{Problem 074: The Collatz Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Collatz map]
The \emph{Collatz map} $T \colon \N \to \N$ is given by $T(n) = n/2$ for even $n$ and $T(n) = 3n + 1$ for odd $n$. A natural number $n$ \emph{reaches $1$} if $T^k(n) = 1$ for some $k \geq 0$, where $T^k$ denotes the $k$-fold iterate of $T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Collatz conjecture]\label{collatz}
Prove or disprove: every positive integer reaches $1$ under iteration of the Collatz map.
\end{openproblem}

The conjecture goes back to Collatz (1937); see the survey of Lagarias \cite{lagarias1985threexplusone}. It is equivalent to the conjunction of two statements: no orbit of a positive integer diverges, and the only cycle of $T$ on the positive integers is $1 \to 4 \to 2 \to 1$. It has been verified for all $n < 2^{68}$ \cite{barina2020convergence}; Tao \cite{tao2019almost} proved that almost all orbits attain almost bounded values; and any nontrivial cycle would have length at least $17\,087\,915$ \cite{eliahou1993cycles}, a bound since improved.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The hypothesis $n > 0$ excludes $0$, which is a fixed point of $T$. Since $k = 0$ is allowed, $n = 1$ reaches $1$ trivially.

