\section*{Problem 075: Bounded Avoidability Index of Patterns}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Patterns and occurrences]
A \emph{pattern} is a finite word $p$ over an alphabet of \emph{variables} $x_1, \dots, x_m$. The pattern $p$ \emph{occurs} in a word $w$ over an alphabet $\Sigma$ if there is a non-erasing substitution $h$, assigning to each variable a nonempty word over $\Sigma$, such that the word $h(p)$, obtained from $p$ by replacing each variable $x$ by $h(x)$, is a factor (a block of consecutive letters) of $w$. An infinite word $w = w_0 w_1 w_2 \cdots$ \emph{avoids} $p$ if $p$ occurs in none of its finite factors.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Avoidability]
A pattern $p$ is \emph{$k$-avoidable} if some infinite word over a $k$-letter alphabet avoids $p$, and \emph{avoidable} if it is $k$-avoidable for some $k$. The \emph{avoidability index} of an avoidable pattern is the least such $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Bounded avoidability index]\label{avoidability}
Prove or disprove: every avoidable pattern is $5$-avoidable.
\end{openproblem}

Currie \cite{currie2005patternavoidance} asked at WORDS 2003 whether there is a pattern that is $6$-avoidable but not $5$-avoidable; the statement above asserts that no avoidable pattern has avoidability index larger than $5$, and whether the avoidability index is bounded at all is open. Zimin \cite{zimin1984blocking} characterised the avoidable patterns: a pattern over $m$ variables is unavoidable if and only if it occurs in the Zimin word $Z_m$, where $Z_1 = x_1$ and $Z_{i+1} = Z_i x_{i+1} Z_i$. Every avoidable pattern over two variables is $3$-avoidable (Cassaigne, Roth), and every doubled pattern, in which each variable occurs at least twice, is $3$-avoidable \cite{ochem2016doubled}. Clark \cite{clark2006avoidability} showed that the pattern $abvacwbaxbcycdazdcd$ has avoidability index exactly $5$, so the bound $5$ cannot be lowered. It is not known whether $k$-avoidability of a given pattern is decidable.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} A pattern over $m$ variables is a list over $\{0, \dots, m-1\}$, and an infinite word over $k$ letters is a map $\N \to \{0, \dots, k-1\}$. The substitution assigns a nonempty word to every variable, including variables that do not occur in $p$, which makes no difference since the alphabet of an infinite word is nonempty.

