\section*{Problem 076: Conway's 99-Graph Problem}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Strongly regular graph]
A finite simple graph is \emph{strongly regular with parameters} $(n, k, \lambda, \mu)$ if it has $n$ vertices, is $k$-regular, any two adjacent vertices have exactly $\lambda$ common neighbours, and any two distinct non-adjacent vertices have exactly $\mu$ common neighbours.
\end{definition}

\begin{openproblem}[Conway's 99-graph problem]\label{conway99}
Prove or disprove: there is a strongly regular graph with parameters $(99, 14, 1, 2)$, that is, a $14$-regular graph on $99$ vertices in which any two adjacent vertices have exactly one common neighbour and any two non-adjacent vertices exactly two.
\end{openproblem}

Conway \cite{conway2014five} offered $\$1000$ for a resolution. Equivalently, one asks for a symmetric $0/1$ matrix $A$ of order $99$ with zero diagonal and $A^2 = 14 I + A + 2(J - I - A)$, where $J$ is the all-ones matrix. The parameters pass every known feasibility condition \cite{brouwervanmaldeghem2022srg}: the eigenvalues $3$ and $-4$ have integral multiplicities $54$ and $44$, and the Krein conditions and the absolute bound hold. In such a graph the neighbourhood of every vertex induces a perfect matching on $14$ vertices, so every edge lies in a unique triangle. Strong restrictions on the automorphism group of a hypothetical graph are known \cite{makhnevminakova2004}. The sibling parameter sets $(9, 4, 1, 2)$ and $(243, 22, 1, 2)$ are realised by the Paley graph on nine vertices and by the Berlekamp--van Lint--Seidel graph.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The vertex set is $\{0, \dots, 98\}$, and the formal statement asks for a graph on it together with a decision procedure for its adjacency relation, which every relation on a finite set admits; strong regularity is Mathlib's \texttt{IsSRGWith}, matching the definition above.

