\section*{Challenge 78: The Borodin--Kostochka Conjecture}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Chromatic number, maximum degree, clique number]
For a finite simple graph $G$, the \emph{chromatic number} $\chi(G)$ is the least number of colours in a proper vertex colouring, the \emph{maximum degree} $\Delta(G)$ is the largest number of neighbours of a vertex, and the \emph{clique number} $\omega(G)$ is the largest size of a set of pairwise adjacent vertices.
\end{definition}

Brooks' theorem states that $\chi(G) \le \max(\Delta(G), \omega(G))$ whenever $\Delta(G) \ge 3$: the only graphs needing $\Delta + 1$ colours are those containing a complete graph $K_{\Delta+1}$. Borodin and Kostochka \cite{borodinkostochka1977} conjectured that the degree term can be lowered by one once the maximum degree is at least $9$; the threshold is sharp, since for $\Delta \le 8$ the Catlin graphs (complements of cycles with all vertices replaced by independent sets) are counterexamples.

\begin{conjecture}[Borodin--Kostochka]\label{bk}
Every finite simple graph $G$ with $\Delta(G) \ge 9$ satisfies
$$\chi(G) \le \max\big(\Delta(G) - 1,\ \omega(G)\big).$$
Equivalently, every graph with $\Delta \ge 9$ and $\omega < \Delta$ is $(\Delta - 1)$-colourable.
\end{conjecture}

Reed \cite{reed1999} proved the conjecture for $\Delta \ge 10^{14}$, and it is known for claw-free graphs and several other classes; Cranston and Rabern \cite{cranstonrabern2013} showed that it is equivalent to a number of seemingly weaker statements. The threshold $9$ is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The vertex set is an arbitrary finite type. The chromatic number is Mathlib's, with values in $\N \cup \{\infty\}$ (finite here, since the graph is finite), and $\Delta(G) - 1$ is computed with truncated subtraction, which is harmless since $\Delta(G) \ge 9$.

