\section*{Challenge 81: Lovász's Hamiltonian-Path Question for Vertex-Transitive Graphs}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Vertex-transitive graphs, Hamiltonian paths]
A finite simple graph $G$ is \emph{vertex-transitive} if its automorphism group acts transitively on the vertices: for any two vertices $u, v$ there is an automorphism $\varphi$ of $G$ with $\varphi(u) = v$. A \emph{Hamiltonian path} is a path visiting every vertex exactly once, i.e. a path with $n$ vertices where $n = |V(G)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{conjecture}[Lovász]\label{lovaszpath}
Every finite connected vertex-transitive graph has a Hamiltonian path.
\end{conjecture}

Lovász \cite{lovasz1970} posed the question in 1969. The stronger statement that every such graph has a Hamiltonian cycle is known to fail for exactly five graphs ($K_2$, the Petersen graph, the Coxeter graph and their two truncations), all of which have Hamiltonian paths, and it is conjectured (Babai, Thomassen) that these are the only exceptions; the Cayley-graph case is a separate well-known conjecture. Long paths and cycles are known: Babai proved that connected vertex-transitive graphs on $n$ vertices have cycles of length $\Omega(\sqrt{3n})$, DeVos improved this to $n^{3/5}$-type bounds, and Groenland, Johnston, Kupavskii, Meeks, Scott and Tan \cite{groenland2025} obtained the current long-cycle bounds; the question is open.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The vertex type is finite, vertex-transitivity is transitivity of the action of the automorphism group $G \simeq_g G$ on the vertices, and the conclusion asks for a path $p$ with $\mathrm{length}(p) + 1 = |V|$; this is the mathlib notion \texttt{Walk.IsHamiltonian}.

