\section*{Problem 88: Elementary Ramsey binomial bound}
\begin{theorem}[Erdős--Szekeres]
Let $r,s\geq 1$ be integers and let $G$ be a finite simple graph. If
$|V(G)|\geq \binom{r+s-2}{r-1}$, then $G$ contains an $r$-vertex clique
or an $s$-vertex independent set.
\end{theorem}

An independent set is a clique in the complement graph. No connectedness
or nonempty-vertex assumption is added: the positive binomial threshold
already excludes the empty graph. When either target is one, the threshold
is one. This is equivalently the classical bound
$R(r,s)\leq\binom{r+s-2}{r-1}$.

