\section*{Challenge 92: The Friendship Theorem}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{theorem}[Erdős--Rényi--Sós; see \cite[Theorem 3.1]{Sason2025Friendship}]
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a nonempty finite simple graph. Suppose that for all distinct $u,v\in V$, there is exactly one $w\in V$ adjacent to both $u$ and $v$. Then there exists $c\in V$ adjacent to every vertex of $V\setminus\{c\}$.
\end{theorem}

The hypothesis applies to adjacent and nonadjacent pairs alike. The one-vertex graph is included; the empty graph is excluded. The conclusion implies the full windmill structure: after deleting $c$, the remaining vertices form disjoint pairs, with exactly the edges joining the paired vertices.

