\section*{Challenge 94: Lossless Vertex Expanders (Existence Form)}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Two-sided vertex expanders]
A bipartite graph with sides $L$, $R$ is $(d_L, d_R)$-biregular if every vertex of $L$ has degree $d_L$ and every vertex of $R$ has degree $d_R$. It is a \emph{two-sided $\gamma$-vertex expander} with density threshold $\eta > 0$ if every $S \subseteq L$ with $|S| \le \eta |L|$ has $|N(S)| \ge \gamma d_L |S|$ and every $T \subseteq R$ with $|T| \le \eta |R|$ has $|N(T)| \ge \gamma d_R |T|$. A family $(Z_n)$ is a two-sided $\gamma$-vertex expander if one threshold $\eta$ works for all its members.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hsieh--Lubotzky--Mohanty--Reiner--Zhang, Theorem 2.2 \cite{hsieh2025lossless}]\label{lossless}
For every $\varepsilon \in (0,1]$ there is $k = k(\varepsilon)$ such that for every $\beta \in (0,1]$ there is $d_0 = d_0(\varepsilon,\beta)$ with the following property. For all $d_L, d_R \ge d_0$ with $\beta \le d_L/d_R \le \beta + \varepsilon$ there is an infinite family of $(k d_L, k d_R)$-biregular bipartite graphs on $\Theta(n)$ vertices which is a two-sided $(1-\varepsilon)$-vertex expander.
\end{theorem}

The paper constructs the family in polynomial time as a tripartite line product of structured bipartite graphs coming from Ramanujan Cayley cubical complexes with a fixed gadget. Footnote 1 of the paper turns $(1-\varepsilon)$-vertex expansion into $(1-2\varepsilon)$-unique-neighbour expansion.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} Only the existence of the family and its expansion are asserted, with the paper's parameters: the clause of Theorem 2.2 that $Z_n$ is output by a $\mathrm{poly}(n)$-time algorithm, and the word \emph{explicit} in Theorem 1 of \cite{hsieh2025lossless}, are statements about a model of computation and are not formalised. What remains is a classical existence theorem: random biregular graphs are two-sided lossless expanders, so existence (even with $k=1$) also follows from the probabilistic method, and the paper's contribution, the explicit construction, is not part of the formal problem. A bipartite graph is given by the sizes of its sides $L=\{0,\dots,n_L-1\}$, $R=\{0,\dots,n_R-1\}$ and an edge relation; ``on $\Theta(n)$ vertices'' means $|L_n|+|R_n|=\Theta(n)$ as $n\to\infty$, and the window $\beta\le d_L/d_R\le\beta+\varepsilon$ is written as $\beta d_R\le d_L\le(\beta+\varepsilon)d_R$, which is equivalent since $d_R>0$. The file also derives two consequences of the theorem: for $\beta=1$ and $d_L=d_R=d$, $kd$-regular $(1-\varepsilon)$-vertex expanders on $L\sqcup R$ with density threshold $\eta/2$ (Theorem 1 of the paper for degrees divisible by $k$; its extension to every $d\ge d_0$, Remark 2.3, is not formalised), and, via Footnote 1, two-sided $(1-2\varepsilon)$-unique-neighbour expanders.

