\section*{Challenge 95: Explicit Two-Sided Unique-Neighbour Expanders}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Unique neighbours, expansion profile]
For a vertex set $S$ of a graph, a \emph{unique neighbour} of $S$ is a vertex adjacent to exactly one member of $S$. A graph has \emph{unique-neighbour expansion profile at least $c$ up to size $t$} if every nonempty set $S$ with $|S| \le t$ has at least $c\,|S|$ unique neighbours.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hsieh--McKenzie--Mohanty--Paredes, Theorem 4.4 \cite{hsieh2023twosided}]\label{twosided}
For every $\beta \in (0, 1/2]$ there exist $d_0$ and $\delta > 0$ such that for all even $d_1 \le d_2$ with $d_1, d_2 \ge d_0$ and $\beta d_2 \le (1-\beta) d_1$ there are arbitrarily large $N$ and bipartite graphs on $N$ vertices, of degree $2d_1$ on one side and $2d_2$ on the other, with unique-neighbour expansion profile at least $\delta d_1$ up to size $N/d_1^3$.
\end{theorem}

The construction is a line product of a bipartite spectral expander with a fixed gadget.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The formal statement is Theorem 4.4 of \cite{hsieh2023twosided}; the strong explicitness of the family and the Cayley-group structure in Theorem 1.2 are properties of the construction that Theorem 4.4 does not assert, and they are not formalised. The graph has vertex set $\{0,\dots,N-1\}$ and a side function to $\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$: every edge joins the two sides, vertices on the side $\mathrm{true}$ have degree $2d_1$ and the others degree $2d_2$; ``arbitrarily large'' means that for every $n_0$ there is such a graph with $N\ge n_0$. A unique neighbour of $S$ is any vertex with exactly one neighbour in $S$, possibly a vertex of $S$ itself. The window $\beta/(1-\beta)\le d_1/d_2\le1$ is written as $d_1\le d_2$ and $\beta d_2\le(1-\beta)d_1$, which is equivalent since $1-\beta\ge1/2>0$, and the size bound $N/d_1^3$ uses floor division, which changes nothing because $|S|$ is an integer.

