\section*{Challenge 105: Perfect Matchings in the Semi-Random Graph Process}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[The semi-random graph process]
In round $t$ a vertex $u_t \in [n]$ is chosen uniformly at random, the player, who sees the current graph and $u_t$, chooses a vertex $v_t$ (possibly at random), and the edge $u_t v_t$ is added. For an increasing graph property $\mathcal P$ and $0 < q < 1$, $\tau_{\mathcal P}(S,q,n)$ is the least $t$ such that the graph after $t$ rounds under strategy $S$ has $\mathcal P$ with probability at least $q$, $\tau_{\mathcal P}(q,n) = \inf_S \tau_{\mathcal P}(S,q,n)$, and $\tau_{\mathcal P} = \lim_{q \to 1^-} \limsup_{n} \tau_{\mathcal P}(q,n)/n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gao--MacRury--Pra\l{}at, Theorem 1.1 \cite{gaomacruryralat2021matchings}]\label{semirandompm}
$\tau_{\mathrm{PM}} \le 1.20524$: for every $q < 1$ and $\varepsilon > 0$ and all large $n$, some strategy builds a perfect matching of $[n]$ (all but one vertex saturated when $n$ is odd) with probability at least $q$ within $(1.20524+\varepsilon)n$ rounds.
\end{theorem}

The constant comes from the differential-equation analysis of the paper's phased algorithm followed by a randomised algorithm and a clean-up phase.

\paragraph{Formalisation notes.} The vertex set is $[n]$ with $n \ge 1$, and $\mathrm{PM}$ is evaluated on the underlying simple graph of the play, loops and repeated edges being discarded. A strategy prescribes, in every round, a probability distribution for $v_t$ that may depend on $u_1, v_1, \dots, u_{t-1}, v_{t-1}$ and $u_t$; any vertex may be chosen, $u_t$ included, and $\tau_{\mathcal P}(q,n)$ is the infimum over all such strategies. $\mathrm{PM}$ is read as the existence of a matching saturating all but at most one vertex, which for even $n$ is a perfect matching. $\tau_{\mathcal P}(S,q,n) = \infty$ if no round reaches probability $q$, and since $q \mapsto \limsup_n \tau_{\mathcal P}(q,n)/n$ is non-decreasing, the limit $q \to 1^-$ is taken as the supremum over $0 < q < 1$; the theorem is the inequality $\tau_{\mathrm{PM}} \le 1.20524$ in $[0,\infty]$. The paper's sharper form $\tau_{\mathrm{PM}} \le \beta + 10^{-5}$ is not stated, as $\beta$ is only determined numerically there, and its lower bound $\tau_{\mathrm{PM}} \ge 0.93261$ (Theorem~1.3 of \cite{gaomacruryralat2021matchings}) is not part of this problem.

