\section*{Problem 109: Two vertices of equal degree}
\refstepcounter{section}
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph on $n \ge 2$ vertices; the \emph{degree} of a vertex is the number of its neighbours.
\begin{theorem}
Some two distinct vertices of $G$ have the same degree.
\end{theorem}
A standard exercise \cite{Bollobas1998MGT, Lovasz1993CPE}: degrees lie in $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, but $0$ and $n-1$ cannot both occur, so $n$ vertices take at most $n-1$ values.

The formal statement uses Mathlib's \texttt{SimpleGraph} with a finite vertex type and \texttt{degree}.

