\section*{Problem 110: A zero-sum block of consecutive integers}
\refstepcounter{section}
\begin{theorem}
For every $n \ge 1$ and every sequence $a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}$ of integers there are indices $0 \le i < j \le n$ with $n \mid a_i + a_{i+1} + \dots + a_{j-1}$.
\end{theorem}
A classical pigeonhole argument \cite{Lovasz1993CPE}: the $n+1$ prefix sums $S_0 = 0, S_1, \dots, S_n$ take at most $n$ values modulo $n$, and the difference of two equal ones is a block sum.  It is a much weaker relative of the Erd\H{o}s--Ginzburg--Ziv theorem \cite{ErdosGinzburgZiv1961}, which is in Mathlib.

The formal statement takes the sequence as a function $\mathbb N \to \mathbb Z$ and sums over the half-open interval $[i, j)$.

