\section*{Problem 111: A consecutive pair among $n+1$ numbers from $\{1,\dots,2n\}$}
\refstepcounter{section}
\begin{theorem}
Every set of $n + 1$ integers from $\{1, 2, \dots, 2n\}$ contains two consecutive integers (which are therefore coprime).
\end{theorem}
A classical pigeonhole exercise, often attributed to Erd\H{o}s \cite{Lovasz1993CPE, AignerZiegler2018Book}: the $n$ pairs $\{2k-1, 2k\}$ partition $\{1,\dots,2n\}$, so two of the $n+1$ numbers lie in the same pair.  The companion statement that two of the numbers divide one another is a separate problem.

The formal statement takes a \texttt{Finset} $S \subseteq [1, 2n]$ with $|S| \ge n+1$ and asserts $a, a+1 \in S$ for some $a$.

