\section*{Challenge 117: Vizing's Theorem, Constructively}
\refstepcounter{section}

\begin{definition}[Proper edge-colouring]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph. A \emph{proper edge-colouring} of $G$ with $k$ colours is a function $c : E\ev{G} \rightarrow [k]$ such that any two edges sharing a common endpoint receive different colours.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Maximum degree]
The \emph{maximum degree} $\Delta\ev{G}$ of $G$ is the largest number of edges incident with any single vertex of $G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Vizing's Theorem \mbox{\cite[Theorem~5.3.2, p.~103]{diestel}}, constructively \cite{misragries1992}]\label{vizing-constructive}
Every finite simple graph $G$ has a proper edge-colouring with $\Delta\ev{G} + 1$ colours.
\end{theorem}

This challenge belongs to the \emph{constructive track}: the proof must not use the axiom of choice. In Lean, it may depend on the axioms \texttt{propext} and \texttt{Quot.sound} but not on \texttt{Classical.choice}, so the law of excluded middle is available only for decidable propositions. The graph is given on the vertices $0, \dots, n-1$ with decidable adjacency, so a proof amounts to an algorithm. Misra and Gries gave such an algorithm: it colours the edges one at a time, rotating a fan of edges at a vertex and swapping two colours along an alternating path where necessary.

